UNSOS FTS Service Desk Assistant — Streamlit Edition
A Streamlit rebuild of the UNSOS FTS Service Desk chatbot, replacing the earlier client-side EmailJS escalation flow with server-side email dispatch via the Microsoft Graph API — the approach that actually works against a real Office 365/Exchange Online mailbox such as `unsos.service@un.org`.
Why not EmailJS or plain SMTP?
Microsoft disabled legacy SMTP AUTH (basic auth) by default for Exchange Online in 2022, and most enterprise/UN tenants layer MFA and Conditional Access on top of that. EmailJS's built-in connectors, and any "SMTP + username + password" approach, depend on exactly the legacy auth path Microsoft has been shutting off. That's almost certainly why a client-side EmailJS flow fails silently or errors out against a mailbox like `unsos.service@un.org`.
The supported modern replacement is Microsoft Graph's app-only (client credentials) OAuth flow: no SMTP, no basic auth, works fine under MFA/Conditional Access, and is the same mechanism enterprise ticketing and mail-merge tools use against O365 today.
What this app does
Same knowledge base and location-aware logic as the original (Wi-Fi, SafeCom printing, Cisco/Teams telephony, FSS/Umoja, Unite ID, MFA, shared mailboxes, Windows activation), ported to Python.
Location intake form (name, email, duty station, building/block), same as before.
Chat interface matching queries against the knowledge base, with a generic first-phase-tips fallback for unmatched queries — so nothing is escalated without troubleshooting shown first.
Escalation dispatch via Microsoft Graph, with:
a visible "Creating ticket..." status while sending,
a real success/failure result (never a false-positive "sent" message),
and a pre-filled manual email template with a copy option shown automatically if the automated send fails, so a request is never silently lost.
Setup
1. Install dependencies
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
2. Get Graph API credentials (requires UN ICT / Exchange admin)
This is not something an individual user can self-provision — it requires an Entra ID app registration and admin consent. Full steps are in `.streamlit/secrets.toml.example`. In short:
Register an app in the tenant's Entra ID.
Grant it Graph Mail.Send (application) permission with admin consent.
Generate a client secret.
Restrict the app to only the `unsos.service@un.org` mailbox using an Exchange Online Application Access Policy (important — without this, the app can send as any mailbox in the tenant).
3. Configure secrets
Copy `.streamlit/secrets.toml.example` to `.streamlit/secrets.toml` and fill in the real `tenant_id`, `client_id`, and `client_secret`. Never commit the real file — it's already in `.gitignore`.
For Streamlit Community Cloud, paste the same keys into the app's Secrets panel in the dashboard instead of using a file.
4. Run it
```bash
streamlit run app.py
```
Graceful degradation
If Graph credentials aren't configured yet (e.g. while UN ICT is still setting up the app registration), the app doesn't crash or silently fail — it shows the manual email template with a copy-ready ticket immediately, so the tool is still usable end-to-end from day one.
Project structure
```
unsos-service-desk-streamlit/
├── app.py
├── requirements.txt
├── .streamlit/
│   └── secrets.toml.example
├── .gitignore
└── README.md
```
